### What is class-level @Transactional?

When `@Transactional` is placed on a class, Spring applies it as the default transaction configuration for every public method in that bean. Every method call through the proxy inherits the class-level attributes: propagation, isolation, readOnly, timeout, and rollbackFor.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class AccountInquiryService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountInquiryService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Inherits readOnly = true from class
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return accountRepo.findById(accountId).getBalance();
    }

    // Inherits readOnly = true from class
    public List<Account> getByType(String type) {
        return accountRepo.findByType(type);
    }

    // Inherits readOnly = true from class
    public long countActive() {
        return accountRepo.countByStatus("ACTIVE");
    }
}
```

All three methods run in a read-only transaction without any annotation on them individually.

### What is method-level @Transactional?

When `@Transactional` is placed on a specific method, it sets the transaction configuration for that method only. If there is also a class-level annotation, the method-level settings completely replace the class-level settings for that method. There is no merging of individual attributes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class DepositService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public DepositService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class, timeout = 10)
    public void deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
        account.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(account);
    }
}
```

### How does precedence work?

Spring resolves which annotation applies through `AnnotationTransactionAttributeSource`. It first checks the method for a `@Transactional` annotation. If found, it uses the method-level settings. If not found, it falls back to the class-level annotation. There is no merging: if the method-level annotation specifies only `rollbackFor`, the other attributes (like readOnly, timeout) take their default values, not the class-level values.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true, timeout = 30)
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Uses class-level: readOnly=true, timeout=30
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return accountRepo.findById(accountId).getBalance();
    }

    // Method-level overrides EVERYTHING: readOnly=false (default), timeout=-1 (default)
    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
        account.debit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(account);
    }

    // Uses class-level: readOnly=true, timeout=30
    public List<Account> getHighBalance(BigDecimal threshold) {
        return accountRepo.findByBalanceGreaterThan(threshold);
    }
}
```

Notice that `withdraw` specifies only `rollbackFor`. It does not inherit the class-level `timeout = 30` because method-level replaces the entire class-level annotation. If you want the timeout on the write method too, you must specify it explicitly: `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class, timeout = 30)`.

### What is the best practice pattern?

The recommended pattern is `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` at the class level and writable `@Transactional` on the specific methods that modify data. This pattern has three advantages:

- Every read method automatically skips Hibernate dirty checking and can be routed to a read replica.
- A developer who adds a new read method does not need to remember the annotation; it is safe by default.
- Write methods are explicitly marked, making the code easy to review.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class SavingsAccountService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public SavingsAccountService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Read methods: inherit class-level readOnly=true
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return accountRepo.findById(accountId).getBalance();
    }

    public List<Account> findByCustomer(String customerId) {
        return accountRepo.findByCustomerId(customerId);
    }

    public long countSavingsAccounts() {
        return accountRepo.countByType("SAVINGS");
    }

    // Write methods: explicit override
    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
        account.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(account);
    }

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class, timeout = 10)
    public void withdraw(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findByIdForUpdate(accountId);
        if (account.getBalance().compareTo(amount) < 0) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException(
                "Account " + accountId + " has insufficient balance");
        }
        account.debit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(account);
    }
}
```

### What about inheritance and interfaces?

A class-level `@Transactional` on a parent class does not automatically apply to a subclass. The subclass must redeclare the annotation. Similarly, `@Transactional` on an interface does not reliably propagate to implementations under CGLIB proxying (Spring Boot default). Always place the annotation on the concrete class.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

// Parent class with class-level annotation
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public abstract class BaseAccountService {
    // read methods here
}

// WRONG: subclass does not inherit the parent's class-level annotation reliably
@Service
public class CurrentAccountService extends BaseAccountService {
    // methods here run WITHOUT readOnly unless re-annotated
}

// CORRECT: redeclare on the subclass
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class CurrentAccountServiceFixed extends BaseAccountService {
    // methods here correctly inherit readOnly = true
}
```

### What about private and package-private methods?

Class-level `@Transactional` applies only to public methods because CGLIB proxying works by overriding methods in a subclass, and only public methods can be overridden across packages. Private, protected, and package-private methods are not proxied, so the class-level annotation has no effect on them.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class ReportService {

    // Public: proxied, readOnly = true applies
    public String generateMonthlyReport(String accountId) {
        return buildReport(accountId);
    }

    // Private: NOT proxied, no transaction
    private String buildReport(String accountId) {
        // This runs without a transaction context even though
        // the class has @Transactional(readOnly = true)
        return "report";
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: assuming method-level inherits class-level attributes

```java
// WRONG assumption: timeout=30 is NOT inherited by the method-level annotation
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true, timeout = 30)
public class LedgerService {

    // Developer expects timeout=30 here, but method-level replaces everything
    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void postEntry(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // timeout is -1 (default), NOT 30
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: specify all needed attributes at method level
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true, timeout = 30)
public class LedgerService {

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class, timeout = 30)
    public void postEntry(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // timeout is now 30 as intended
    }
}
```

#### Mistake 2: class-level writable when most methods are reads

```java
// WRONG: every method is writable, no read optimization
@Service
@Transactional
public class AccountServiceBad {
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String id) { ... }  // writable TX for a read
    public List<Account> findAll() { ... }            // writable TX for a read
    public void deposit(String id, BigDecimal amt) { ... } // correctly writable
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: default to readOnly, override for writes
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public class AccountServiceGood {
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String id) { ... }  // readOnly, optimized
    public List<Account> findAll() { ... }            // readOnly, optimized

    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void deposit(String id, BigDecimal amt) { ... } // writable
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The "no merging" rule is the most common source of bugs. When a method-level annotation overrides the class level, it replaces the entire transaction definition, not just the specified attributes.
- Spring uses `AnnotationTransactionAttributeSource.computeTransactionAttribute()` to resolve precedence. Set `logging.level.org.springframework.transaction.interceptor=TRACE` to see which annotation Spring picks for each call.
- In a service with many read methods and few writes, the class-level readOnly pattern reduces annotation noise and ensures new methods are safe by default.
- The class-level readOnly flag works with `AbstractRoutingDataSource` to route all read methods to the replica automatically, reducing primary database load at National Bank of India.

```java
// Enable TRACE logging to see which TX attributes Spring picks
// application.yml
// logging:
//   level:
//     org.springframework.transaction.interceptor: TRACE
```

